-A sample of the data we collect and metrics we compute (obtained via RPC):
+Reachable counts (`num_good_nodes`, `node_addrs`, `node_info`, and `all_node_reachable`) require both a completed version/verack handshake and a successful same-session Zcash mainnet header challenge. The crawler sends `getheaders` with the pinned mainnet height 3,520,000 parent locator and checkpoint stop hash, verifies exactly the expected header hash and parent, and only then requests and accepts address gossip. Protocol magic, ports, advertised chain heights, and software names do not establish chain identity. Client and protocol breakdowns use this same qualified population.
+
+Each qualified `node_info` entry exports the actual handshake `last_verified_at_ms`, actual header proof `chain_verified_at_ms`, pinned `chain_block_hash`, and versioned `chain_proof` marker. A new connection attempt clears prior chain eligibility; unsolicited or repeated headers cannot refresh it. Proofs expire after one hour, and unanswered challenges time out after 30 seconds. A non-answer remains unverified rather than being labeled a different chain. Initial-sync nodes can decline header requests, and pruning does not remove retained headers.
+
+This census measures recently observed reachable endpoints with the pinned mainnet history through height 3,520,000. Known shared-history forks that diverged before this checkpoint fail the challenge; future forks sharing it require a reviewed checkpoint update in both crawler and consumers. It does not establish current tip synchronization, honest behavior, unique physical nodes, Tor onion coverage, or complete network coverage. Historical handshake-only snapshots are incompatible with the new census and must not be used as trend baselines.
+
+The following legacy sample illustrates the RPC shape; its values predate chain-proof qualification and are not current measurements:
 
